On Mon, Jan 21, 2019 at 10:58 PM Hugo Harada <hugo....@adaptativa.com.br> wrote:

> Hello all,
>
> I am trying to write a longitudinal irt model like below. It has three tests applied at different times with common items across them. Theta among the different time is correlated.
>
>
>
> I wrote my mirt model like below using the constraint keyword to indicate anchor itens.
>
> model <- mirt.model(' T1 = 1-30
>   T2 = 31-60
>   T3 = 61-90
>   CONSTRAIN = (21-30, a1), (31-40, a1)
>   CONSTRAIN = (21-30, d), (31-40, d)
>   CONSTRAIN = (51-60, a1), (61-70, a1)
>   CONSTRAIN = (51-60, d), (61-70, d)
>   COV = T1*T2*T3'
> )

Many of these constraints don't exist because they do not have freely estimated slopes. Based on the T1-T3 definitions you should see a1 parameters for items 1 through 30, a2 for items 31 to 60, and T3 for items 61 through 90. So constraints like (31-40, a1) don't work because these parameters are not freely estimated.

>
> mirt.2PL = mirt(U, model, itemtype = '2PL')  #
>
> I get the following error. Where is my mistake? Any ideas?
>
> Error: Constraint applied to fixed parameter(s) 181 187 193 199 205 211 217 223 229 235 301 307 313 319 325 331 337 343 349 355 361 367 373 379 385 391 397 403 409 415  but should only be applied to
>                  estimated parameters. Please fix!
