Nevermind, I found what I was looking for:

Templin's notes on IRT <https://jonathantemplin.com/teaching/#1546295939991-6ff3016d-db4f> recommend looking at standardized residuals of an item to examine fit. There is a way to do this in the mirt framework:

##Dichotomous item response data: 1500 responses on 47 multiple-choice items marked as right (1) or wrong (0)
ItemDat <-read.csv("FMCE_Dat.csv")

##Fit a unidimensional 2PL model
mod1 <- mirt(ItemDat,1,'2PL')

##Extract information for item 1 and abilities
extr.1.1 <- extract.item(mod1,1)
Theta <- fscores(mod1)[,1]

##Compute standard error:
SE.1.1 <- sqrt(probtrace(extr.1.1,Theta)[,2]*probtrace(extr.1.1,Theta)[,1]/length(probtrace(extr.1.1,Theta)[,1]))

##Compute standard residuals:

SR.1.1 <- (ItemDat[,1]-expected.item(extr.1.1,Theta))/SE.1.1

##Plot standardized residuals to examine heteroskedacitiy:

plot(Theta,SR.1.1)

